00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
we begin the day as joe biden prepares  to mark one year in the white house  the leader of the world's sole  superpower is trying to hold together a  world that's about to lose its balance  of power  if you're thinking of threats from a  rising china or a nuclear iran  don't forget a resurrecting russia too  moscow is making demands on washington  and nato to pull back and to stay back  no more eastward expansion no nato  membership for ukraine a return to  soviet-like satellites orbiting the  russian mothership  high-level talks last week between the  u.s and russia nato and russia and the  osce in russia ended with no progress  instead the osce's head warned the  threat of war in europe's backyard is  dangerous and real german foreign  minister analina bearbach's visit to  kiev today sent a message to moscow we  will not act as if the berlin wall never  fell  fears of a russian invasion of ukraine  have jangled nerves in the region  after visiting a memorial to  anti-government protesters who died in  2014 and elena bearbach says any  solution to the conflict must come  through dialogue not arms  meeting ukraine's foreign minister  dimitro culeba bierbach offered an  explicit gesture of support for ukraine  solidarity  solidarity  european solidarity for the sovereignty  and territorial integrity of ukraine  that is not up for debate not for us and  not for me personally any further  aggression would have a high price  we've emphasized that today once again  russia is building up its troops on the  ukrainian border  both the european union and the us have  threatened to impose further harsh  sanctions if russia goes ahead with an  invasion  for years ukraine has called on germany  to deliver arms to help boost its  abilities to defend itself kuleba says  russia doesn't share ukraine's  cooperative attitude towards diffusing  the tensions  we have a very constructive stance on  this matter  but unfortunately  our constructiveness isn't mutual on  russia's part  from kiev bierbach goes to moscow on  tuesday where she'll meet russian  foreign minister sergey lavrov  she's keen to revive four-way peace  talks in the normandy format which  includes germany france ukraine and  russia  well my first guest tonight is constanza  steltzen mueller she's an international  relations analyst and she holds the  fritz stand chair at the brookings  institution she joins us tonight from  washington constance it's good to have  you  on the day  i want to begin by  asking you as someone who comes from  germany who experienced those winds of  change back in 1989  is vladimir putin is he asking europe  and the world with his demands to act as  if the berlin wall never fell  hi brent and thanks for having me on um  thanks for reminding me of my age as  well but no you're quite right um  the  i i think that  to anybody who read the two draft  agreements that the kremlin put in front  of the white house and nato  uh just before christmas had to conclude  that what the kremlin is pursuing here  what vladimir putin is pursuing here is  no longer just  neutrality or finlandization for ukraine  but something much much larger and it is  really the rolling back of the  democratic transformation  of the former countries of the warsaw  pact that are many of whom are now  members of the eu and nato  that's a really big deal  it is a big deal and when you consider  what is at stake  is germany  is europe's voice  are they as loud as they should be in  these discussions i mean i i'm wondering  are washington and moscow talking over  berlin and brussels  yeah there's two parts to this question  here uh the question is are the  americans trying to listen to us and do  we have anything to say  on the former point i can tell you and  you probably have seen it yourself uh  the white house about a week ago  published a list of their phone calls  and meetings with  senior politicians across europe and i  think it had about 100 entries  so i think that question is fairly  summarily answered  um  the unfortunate  answer to your second question is that  it seems to me that the signaling coming  from berlin and then from europe overall  um is pretty confusing  so in berlin you have uh annalina  baybach who's as you said heading to  moscow tonight for meetings tomorrow  um who has said very clearly as far as  i'm concerned nordstrom 2 the pipeline  is on the table  last week several senior social  democrats coalition partners um the  party of olaf schwarz the chancellor  disagreed in public  and the defense minister did so on the  day  that the buy administration was trying  to defeat a  senate vote tabled by senator cruz to  sanction the pipeline in other words the  mind administration has been bending  over backwards and we haven't exactly  been um  shall we say first coming  and at the same time you have christian  democrat politicians as well saying oh  you know we shouldn't we shouldn't have  economic sanctions like um taking russia  out of the payment system swift it's  confusing  well how do you explain germany's  reluctance  to say we're going to kill nordstream 2  because it would obviously it would hurt  vladimir putin it would hit him where it  hurts but they've been reluctant i mean  this is something angela merkel didn't  want to do and now we've got olaf  schultz basically you know carrying on  that that miracle legacy  well um let me walk you back on that um  the chancellor merkel and and u.s  president biden in the summer  came to an agreement  on um bilateral  cooperation on ukraine  which said very clearly that um  energy security and and pipelines uh  would be on the table if  uh russia committed any act of  aggression against ukraine  that was read um as including nordstrom  2  and  also i think more importantly  politically it kind of meant that you  know whatever happens to ukraine germany  in europe now owns that problem  um  schultz has been  quite ambiguous about his commitment  on this point  um the most generous interpretation i  can think of  is that he and his allies in the  government want to preserve strategic  ambiguity about consequences that  wouldn't per se be a mistake but i do  think given the truly astounding  language that's coming out of the  kremlin these days basically saying you  know we're not going to negotiate these  agreements with you you can sign them  that's you know that's it and we're not  going to talk arms control or trust  building measures with you  or foreign minister sergey lavrov whom  bairbuck will meet tomorrow saying  um  you know after 1991  these countries the former countries of  the warsaw pact were orphaned  territories these are countries that are  now members of most of them members of  the eu and nato  um and i think when you have the  rhetoric ratcheting up like this and an  arms build up like you have on ukraine's  eastern border i think it's time for  europeans and specifically the germans  who play a central role here to be more  articulate in their language with the  the german foreign minister i know she  wants to revive the old normandy peace  uh format  which  you know obviously has france and  germany as mediators between moscow and  kiev  do you think it's is it too late for  that  i think i do i think uh i think that  this is  really buying time and i think everybody  in berlin knows that i think the foreign  minister knows that that's again i think  not illegitimate  but i think that is what we're doing  here um the question really is does  anybody have a way  to um  sort of get the kremlin down from the  extremely high flagpole on which it has  uh positioned itself  that's really hard to see again given  the military buildup that's already  there  recent increases in recent days  um and the really astonishingly  uncompromising belligerent rhetoric  um it's hard to see how the kremlin does  that without losing face and we know  that's not something it likes to do  and what about the options for the west  moving forward if going to war over  ukraine  is out of the question  should the u.s  nato should they arm ukraine heavily  enough  so that it can at least make  another russian incursion at least  uncomfortable and ugly for the kremlin  well i mean the us and the uk have been  doing that  already  and i think the ukrainians on that point  are fairly relaxed i mean there are some  things that they haven't received such  as i don't know patriot missile defenses  um  or manpad stingers but at least that's  what i'm reading  um  but  i think the ukrainians are relatively  well equipped and trained to fend off  anything sort of short of a massive  full-scale invasion it's still not clear  that that's what the russians  are um  gearing up for  um  i think the more important  thing to understand here is that for  anybody who has like me since you did  mention my age lived through the  yugoslav wars of uh the 1990s when i was  a reporter for insight the weekly paper  that was a decade-long war  and a slow unraveling  of the yugoslav federal republic  into  military mayhem war crimes and genocide  and the the risk that i'm seeing  is that you could see a protracted  extended conflict with sort of unclear  um battle lines um  atrocities being committed  um  culpability perhaps on both sides you  never know in combat  and then you have  you have a humanitarian disaster you  have refugee flows  you have  thousands tens of thousands of  frightened people fleeing and needing  help  and then i think there comes a moral and  perhaps a legal debate in the west about  helping  civilian societies on the battlefield  and whether those might need and the  helpers and and the and the and the  people being under attack  um need some sort of military protection  and i think  the professionals  know exactly that that is a possibility  but that is a moral and legal and  political morass that nobody i think  really at this point wants to discuss  publicly let me get one more question in  before we run out of time i just want to  broaden this out before we say goodbye  the us is preoccupied with russia and  ukraine efforts to revive the iran  nuclear deal as well  china has to be watching this and taking  notes  uh i'm wondering is this going to have  any bearing for the future of taiwan  well clearly  the kremlin and the and and beijing are  looking at this and trying to figure out  how resolute  um  the  the by administration is  how  durable and sustainable alliance  cohesion is and whether there are chinks  in in the carapace of the alliance  within europe or within european  capitals  and of course in this context the sort  of messaging confusion and reluctance  that i was talking about earlier in the  context of the berlin debate  is unhelpful because of the key role  that berlin has to play in mustering  european cohesion  and so and  not just because of the pipeline  and and so  that is i think unfortunate not just  because it could be seen as some  commentators have suggested as  encouraging or even enabling the kremlin  but also sending signals farther abroad  and  that might be something which we would  regret greatly at a later point say if  if there is a chinese move against  taiwan  let's not forget that apart from being  an asian democracy taiwan is also one of  the world's largest producers of  semi-conductors that's right and sits on  a very important sea lane  unfortunately consensus we're out of  time  i wish we had more time constantly still  the timeless  constances it's good having you on the  show please come back again i'm sure  there's going to be plenty to to talk  about between you and me us old-timers  thank you  absolutely

